In a **fixed-size window** of size `k`, the window moves one step right every iteration. The left pointer is always `right - k`. The state update is: add `arr[right]`, remove `arr[right - k]`.

## Example: Maximum Sum Subarray of Size k

```javascript
function maxSumFixed(arr, k) {
  let windowSum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) windowSum += arr[i]; // initial window
  let best = windowSum;
  for (let right = k; right < arr.length; right++) {
    windowSum += arr[right] - arr[right - k]; // add right element, remove leftmost
    best = Math.max(best, windowSum);
  }
  return best;
}
```

The key line is `windowSum += arr[right] - arr[right - k]`. Instead of summing `k` elements each time (O(k) per step → O(nk) total), we make one addition and one subtraction - O(1) per step → O(n) total.

## When to Use Fixed Window

Whenever the problem specifies 'exactly size k' or 'subarray of length k'. Also used in string problems: 'find all anagrams of p in s' uses a fixed window of size `p.length`.

In a **variable-size window**, the right edge advances every outer loop iteration. The left edge advances only when the window becomes invalid (violates the constraint). The window size can grow and shrink.

## The Template Reasoning

Right advances unconditionally - it expands the window to include the new element. Then we check: is the window valid? If not, advance left (and update state to reflect the removal) until it is valid again. After the shrink step, the window is guaranteed to be the largest valid window ending at `right`.

## Example: Longest Substring with At Most 2 Distinct Characters

```javascript
function atMost2Distinct(s) {
  const freq = new Map();
  let left = 0, best = 0;
  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    freq.set(s[right], (freq.get(s[right]) || 0) + 1);  // expand
    while (freq.size > 2) {                               // constraint violated
      const lc = s[left++];
      freq.set(lc, freq.get(lc) - 1);
      if (freq.get(lc) === 0) freq.delete(lc);           // shrink until valid
    }
    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
  }
  return best;
}
```

## Why Left Doesn't Go Back

A key property: once `left` advances, it never retreats. This is what guarantees O(n) - `left` and `right` each traverse the array at most once.

